# ⚙️ Installation & Setup

 1. Clone the Repository

bash
git clone https://github.com/JissmonJoshy/Django-Celery-Redis-Projects.git

cd Django-Celery-Redis-Projects/shopify-product-taxonomy-classifier


 2. Create a Virtual Environment

# Windows

bash
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate


# macOS / Linux

bash
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate


 3. Install Dependencies

bash
pip install django djangorestframework mysqlclient openpyxl scikit-learn celery redis requests


Alternatively, if a `requirements.txt` file is included:

bash
pip install -r requirements.txt


 4. Create MariaDB Database

sql
CREATE DATABASE shopify_classifier
CHARACTER SET utf8mb4;


Configure the database credentials in:

text
shop_prj/settings.py


 5. Configure Environment Variables

# Windows

cmd
set EMAIL_HOST_USER=your_email@gmail.com
set EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD=your_app_password


For production deployments, use a `.env` file or secure environment-variable management instead of committing credentials to Git.

 6. Start Redis

Redis can be started using Docker:

bash
docker run -d --name redis -p 6379:6379 redis


Verify the container:

bash
docker ps


 7. Apply Database Migrations

bash
python manage.py migrate


Create an administrator:

bash
python manage.py createsuperuser


 8. Load Shopify Taxonomy Categories

bash
python manage.py seed_categories


 9. Start Django

bash
python manage.py runserver


 10. Start Celery Worker

Open another terminal and activate the virtual environment:

bash
celery -A shop_prj worker --loglevel=info --pool=solo


The application and Celery worker should now be running together.



# 🚀 Usage

 Web Application

| Feature            | URL                    |
|  | - |
| Login              | `/login/`              |
| Admin Dashboard    | `/admin_dashboard/`    |
| Reviewer Dashboard | `/reviewer_dashboard/` |
| Import Products    | `/import-products/`    |
| Product Results    | `/products/`           |
| Product Review     | `/products/<id>/`      |
| Run Classification | `/run-classification/` |

 REST API

| Method  | Endpoint                             | Purpose                |
| - |  | - |
| `GET`   | `/api/classifications/`              | List classifications   |
| `GET`   | `/api/classifications/<id>/`         | View classification    |
| `POST`  | `/api/classifications/<id>/approve/` | Approve classification |
| `PATCH` | `/api/classifications/<id>/`         | Update classification  |



# 🖥️ Management Commands

Import products:

bash
python manage.py import_products


Load taxonomy:

bash
python manage.py seed_categories


Run synchronous classification:

bash
python manage.py classify_products


Detect attributes:

bash
python manage.py detect_attributes


Validate product images:

bash
python manage.py validate_images




# 🔐 User Roles

 Admin

The administrator manages the overall classification pipeline.

text
Admin
 │
 ├── Import Products
 ├── Seed Taxonomy
 ├── Start Classification
 ├── Monitor Results
 └── Manage System Data


 Reviewer

Reviewers handle uncertain AI predictions.

text
Reviewer
 │
 ├── View Pending Reviews
 ├── Inspect Product
 ├── Compare Suggested Categories
 ├── Approve Prediction
 └── Correct Category


This separation helps keep automated processing and human verification as distinct stages.



# 🔄 Human-in-the-Loop Workflow

The system does not treat every AI prediction as final.

text
             AI Classification
                    │
                    ▼
             Confidence Score
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          │                   │
       High                 Low
     Confidence          Confidence
          │                   │
          ▼                   ▼
      Accepted          Manual Review
                              │
                    ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                    │                   │
                 Approve             Correct
                    │                   │
                    └─────────┬─────────┘
                              ▼
                      Final Classification


This allows automation to handle straightforward products while uncertain cases can be reviewed by a human.



# 🧩 Key Design Decisions

# Why TF-IDF?

TF-IDF was selected as a lightweight and explainable baseline for text classification.

Advantages include:

* No GPU requirement
* Fast inference
* Simple implementation
* Easy debugging
* Interpretable similarity-based matching
* Suitable for an initial taxonomy classification pipeline

# Why Celery + Redis?

Synchronous processing becomes problematic when processing thousands of products.

Instead:

text
Web Request
     ↓
Create Background Task
     ↓
Redis
     ↓
Celery Worker
     ↓
Process Products
     ↓
Update Database


The Django application remains available while the classification job runs independently.

# Why MariaDB?

MariaDB provides persistent relational storage for:

* Products
* Taxonomy categories
* Classification results
* Attributes
* Review information
* Processing status

# Why Human Review?

Automated classification can produce uncertain results, especially when product information is incomplete or categories are closely related.

The review workflow provides a controlled way to verify and correct those predictions.



# 🛡️ Fault-Tolerance Strategy

The processing pipeline is designed so that one problematic product does not stop the entire batch.

Example:

text
Product 001 → ✅ Classified
Product 002 → ✅ Classified
Product 003 → ⚠️ Missing description
Product 004 → ✅ Classified
Product 005 → ⚠️ Broken image
Product 006 → ✅ Classified


Instead of terminating the entire job, problematic records can be logged and marked for further processing.



# 📈 Scalability

The architecture is designed with large catalogues in mind.

For example:

text
10,000+ Products
       │
       ▼
Database
       │
       ▼
Celery Queue
       │
 ┌─────┼─────┐
 ▼     ▼     ▼
Worker Worker Worker
  │     │     │
  └─────┼─────┘
        ▼
     MariaDB


Additional Celery workers can be introduced when processing requirements increase.



# 🧪 Testing & API Development

The REST API can be tested using Postman.

Example workflow:

text
POST /api/classifications/
        ↓
Create / Process Data
        ↓
GET /api/classifications/
        ↓
View Results
        ↓
POST /api/classifications/1/approve/
        ↓
Approve Classification



